  "summary": "The foundational technique of exaggerating physical and political characteristics in editorial caricature to create immediate visual commentary.",
  "description": "This style appears deliberately distorted yet recognizable, with emphasized features that serve to highlight personality traits, political positions, or social issues. It developed as newspapers became mass media, needing instantly recognizable visual commentary that could communicate complex political situations to a broad audience.",
  "culturalContext": "Editorial caricature — the exaggeration engine from James Gillray and Honoré Daumier through Thomas Nast, David Levine's NYRB giants and modern KAL/Steve Bell — builds political portraiture from deformed salience: the oversized nose, the tiny eyes, the head-body ratio inversion. Physical caricature theory (Wolpert's \"superportrait\" research) shows exaggeration aids recognition; the genre's ink-hatching craft and political risk (Charlie Hebdo's martyrdom) keep it editorial page's sharpest tool.",
